A cyberattack this week caused refrigerators at a Selver supermarket to heat up to 50 degrees Celsius. Similar incidents were reported in 11 European countries, raising concerns about the vulnerability of essential everyday systems.
The cyberthreat landscape has changed significantly over the past year. Launching a cyberattack no longer requires advanced hacking skills, as artificial intelligence is increasingly being used to identify security vulnerabilities and write malicious software. As a result, attacks can be planned faster and with less expertise than before.
"Cyberattackers have to do quite complex thinking. They experiment and try different things, but can only accomplish so much. Now, with AI helping them at every step, it can do everything 10 times faster and much more effectively than a human. Almost anyone who wants to launch an attack…
